WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.425
After every picture, you turn the green winder,

2
00:00:03.175 --> 00:00:06.090
and one picture's length of film moves along.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.015
Move the film too little, and two pictures land on top of each other.

4
00:00:10.765 --> 00:00:13.897
Move it too much, and you're throwing film away.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.535
And there's no motor in here, and no chip.

6
00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:21.598
So how does the camera know it has moved the film exactly one picture?

7
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.470
Most people say: count turns of the winder.

8
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.475
One turn, one picture.

9
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:29.920
And that would work, except for one thing.

10
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:34.355
The film winds onto a roll, and the roll gets thicker with every turn.

11
00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:37.917
A thicker roll takes in more film each time round.

12
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.652
When it's empty, one turn takes in a little under one picture.

13
00:00:41.402 --> 00:00:45.827
When it's full, one turn takes in about one and two-thirds.

14
00:00:45.577 --> 00:00:48.222
So turns can't count pictures.

15
00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:50.677
The camera has to measure the film itself.

16
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.160
And the film gives it a way.

17
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.790
Look along the edges.

18
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.288
There's a row of holes running down each one, top and bottom.

19
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.090
Those holes have a history.

20
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.130
Around 1892, in Thomas Edison's laboratory,

21
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:11.354
William Dickson settled on film 35 millimetres wide, with holes down both edges.

22
00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:13.415
It was made for moving pictures,

23
00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:17.919
where teeth in the holes pulled the film along by exactly the right amount.

24
00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:23.102
On today's film, those holes are 4.75 millimetres apart.

25
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:30.538
Then in 1913, Oskar Barnack designed a small camera that ran that same film sideways,

26
00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:32.338
to take still pictures.

27
00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:38.823
His picture was 36 by 24 millimetres, and yours is exactly the same size.

28
00:01:38.573 --> 00:01:42.286
It went on sale in 1925 as the Leica.

29
00:01:42.036 --> 00:01:44.696
Now watch the numbers click together.

30
00:01:44.446 --> 00:01:50.731
One picture is 36 millimetres long, and the gap after it is 2, which makes 38.

31
00:01:50.481 --> 00:01:56.661
And eight holes, 4.75 apart, come to exactly 38 millimetres.

32
00:01:56.411 --> 00:01:58.702
Eight holes, one picture.

33
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:02.303
So the camera counts holes, not turns.

34
00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:04.753
That's the job of the blue frame wheel.

35
00:02:04.503 --> 00:02:09.056
It has eight teeth, and they sit in the holes along the film's top edge.

36
00:02:08.806 --> 00:02:10.991
The wheel doesn't pull the film.

37
00:02:10.741 --> 00:02:13.926
The film, as it moves, turns the wheel.

38
00:02:13.676 --> 00:02:18.956
Eight holes go past, the wheel turns once, and the arrow on it comes back home.

39
00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:20.790
The camera doesn't stop you.

40
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.305
You stop, when the arrow comes round.

41
00:02:23.055 --> 00:02:27.508
Each tooth tapers so that it finds its hole, and by the model,

42
00:02:27.258 --> 00:02:31.031
with thin film or thick, no tooth touches the edge of a hole.

43
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:36.203
To load it, you wind the whole film out of its shell onto the red spool.

44
00:02:35.953 --> 00:02:41.985
That takes about 25 turns for 24 pictures, or 33 for 36.

45
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.600
Then, after each picture, the green winder winds it back into the shell.

46
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.025
Why backwards?

47
00:02:48.775 --> 00:02:54.279
Because the shell is dark inside, so the pictures you've already taken are safe in there.

48
00:02:54.029 --> 00:02:56.705
If the back comes open by accident,

49
00:02:56.455 --> 00:03:00.554
the only pictures you lose are the ones you haven't taken yet.

50
00:03:00.304 --> 00:03:04.755
The shell you can load in daylight arrived in 1934.

51
00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:06.882
Now a sum.

52
00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:13.962
One turn of a roll takes in the distance round it: two, times 3.14, times its radius.

53
00:03:13.712 --> 00:03:18.662
The red spool, empty, has a radius of 5.9 millimetres,

54
00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:22.022
so one turn takes in about 37 millimetres.

55
00:03:21.772 --> 00:03:24.027
That's just under one picture.

56
00:03:23.777 --> 00:03:31.977
Full, after 36 pictures, the radius is 10.1, and one turn takes in about 63 millimetres.

57
00:03:31.727 --> 00:03:36.122
63 divided by 37 is 1.7.

58
00:03:35.872 --> 00:03:41.062
The last turn moves 1.7 times as much film as the first.

59
00:03:40.812 --> 00:03:44.132
And the same thing is happening inside the film's shell,

60
00:03:43.882 --> 00:03:48.097
where the roll the green winder winds grows with every picture.

61
00:03:47.847 --> 00:03:52.097
Same winder, same turn, very different lengths.

62
00:03:51.847 --> 00:03:54.024
Only the holes stay the same.

63
00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:59.548
In your kit, the frame wheel lies in a fork on the back, beside a round post.

64
00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:04.156
Close the back, and its teeth come down onto the film's top edge.

65
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:09.572
After the last picture, wind the green winder until the arrow stops moving.

66
00:04:09.322 --> 00:04:11.657
Then give it three more turns.

67
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:16.244
Now all of the film is back in its shell, and the back can come off.

68
00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:21.604
And while you wind, leave the red winder alone, and don't force either one.

69
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:23.693
So how does it know?

70
00:04:23.443 --> 00:04:26.003
It counts holes, not turns.

71
00:04:25.753 --> 00:04:29.603
Eight holes, 38 millimetres, one turn of the wheel.

72
00:04:29.353 --> 00:04:32.568
You can see the problem with turns on a roll of tape:

73
00:04:32.318 --> 00:04:35.482
its outer turns are longer than its inner ones.

74
00:04:35.232 --> 00:04:39.208
And try this: wind a long strip of paper round a pencil,

75
00:04:38.958 --> 00:04:42.277
mark each turn, then unroll it and measure.

76
00:04:42.027 --> 00:04:45.402
So the film moves by exactly one picture.

77
00:04:45.152 --> 00:04:48.573
But that film is sitting in a box with two winders,

78
00:04:48.323 --> 00:04:51.244
a spinning wheel and a back that comes off.

79
00:04:50.994 --> 00:04:55.154
Next time: how can a box full of gaps be dark inside?

80
00:04:54.904 --> 00:04:59.074
Every picture here is drawn from the model or simulated.

81
00:04:58.824 --> 00:05:02.129
No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

